const e="nga-kapa-haka-kura-tuarua",h=2026,a="There is no published rulebook for Ngā Kapa Haka Kura Tuarua o Aotearoa: kapahaka.nz did not resolve and the Te Matatini event pages say 'More information to come'. This file follows the Te Matatini National Festival rules (Ngā Ture o te Whakataetae ā-Motu, approved January 2026), as the template does, and marks each senior-only rule. Printed but not stated for kura tuarua and therefore kept only as short lines under page section 'other': the regional qualification calendar and ratio, 24 to 40 performers, age 14 and over, the 30 percent secondary-school cap, composite kapa haka and performer interchange rules. No age limit, school roll, group size, entry fee, entry deadline, contact person or phone number is printed for the secondary schools event, so eligibility is left empty and edition facts (2026 dates, venue, host, format) are Variables. The named taonga (trophies given by sponsors or in memory of people) and the Te Matatini Kaiwhiriwhiri selection details beyond the judging section are not listed. Pool and finals structure (four pools, Top 12) is the senior festival's; the 2026 secondary schools format printed by the sponsor is preliminaries Tuesday to Thursday and finals on Friday, with no number of finalists printed there. Rule 4.6.3 names the pools Te Ihu, Te Kei, Te Tauihu and Te Awa while Part A names Te Ihu, Te Haumi, Te Kei and Te Awa; Part A is used. The Part A tie-break and rule 4.6.3 are both written.",t=JSON.parse(`{"Title":"Rules & Criteria","Subtitle":"Ngā Kapa Haka Kura Tuarua o Aotearoa, the national secondary schools kapa haka competition. Read these rules before you register.","NoticeTitle":"Please Read Before Registering","Notice":"The secondary schools competition does not publish its own rulebook. The disciplines, scoring, time, script and conduct rules below are those of the Te Matatini National Festival, which this template follows. Eligibility (age, group size, school roll) for the secondary schools event is not printed. Check it with your regional organiser. This edition: {{EventDates}} at {{Venue}}.","Notes":["All seven compulsory disciplines must be finished within 25 minutes.","A full script (lyrics) of every item must reach the organiser at least 20 clear days before the competition starts.","Be ready at the performers' holding area 10 minutes before your performance time.","Edition format: {{EditionFormat}}."],"Sections":[{"Key":"general","Title":"General Rules","Items":["Kapa haka (Māori performing arts groups) take part in this competition. A kapa haka is called a rōpū.","Te Matatini Society Incorporated is the charitable body that fosters, develops and protects kapa haka. These competition rules come from the Te Matatini National Festival, which the secondary schools competition follows. The secondary schools event does not publish its own ture (rules).","Every kapa haka performs seven compulsory disciplines (Ngā Wāhanga Me Mātua Tutuki): Whakaeke, Mōteatea, Waiata ā-ringa, Poi, Haka, Whakawātea and Te Kairangi o Te Mita o Te Reo. These are the aggregate disciplines that decide the placing.","Waiata Tira is an optional discipline. It is not part of the aggregate.","The leaders and delegates must know the competition rules and clear any rule with the National Office before entering a regional competition or the national event.","Regional competitions are held in each rohe (region) to select the kapa haka that go to the national event."]},{"Key":"registration","Title":"Registration, Scripts and Notices","Items":["Each kapa haka must gain selection for the national event through a regional competition held by its rohe.","A full and complete kapa haka script (lyrics) needed for judging must go to the National Office by 12:00 midday, 20 clear days before the competition start date, or by the deadline the National Office gives. A late script loses 2 points from the aggregate total in the pool competition.","Supporting material (such as explanations, interpretations and signed documents of originality) follows the same deadline. Late material loses 2 points from the aggregate total in the pool competition.","Only the National Office sends scripts and supporting material to the judges (kaiwhiriwhiri).","Material received after the due date and time takes the late penalty. There is no guarantee that it reaches the judges in time or is accepted by them.","After the due date, no marks are taken off for small changes to scripts, but there is no guarantee that the changes reach the judges in time.","The Leaders Declaration of Compliance form must be received by the end of the regional competition day. It must also cover composite kapa haka performers.","Each rohe emails its completed form listing the kapa haka that qualified to the Te Matatini National Office within one month after its regional competition.","The official draw for a regional competition must go to the National Office before that competition starts."]},{"Key":"dates","Title":"Dates","Items":["This edition: {{EventDates}}.","Edition format: {{EditionFormat}}.","Results are provisional once they are announced in public. Results from the national competition are final 24 hours after the competition end date."]},{"Key":"venue","Title":"Venue","Items":["Venue: {{Venue}}.","Host for this edition: {{Host}}."]},{"Key":"levels","Title":"Rounds and Qualification","Items":["Qualification: a kapa haka is selected for the national event through a regional competition held by its rohe.","This edition: {{EditionFormat}}.","Te Matatini National Festival, Pool Competition: kapa haka compete across four pools (Te Ihu, Te Haumi, Te Kei and Te Awa), spread equally. Each kapa haka performs in the order of the competition draw.","Te Matatini National Festival: the three kapa haka with the highest aggregate scores in each pool go to the finals, Te Matangirua. In Te Matangirua, kapa haka are judged afresh to choose Te Toa Whakaihuwaka, the overall aggregate winner.","Te Matatini National Festival: twelve kapa haka compete in Te Matangirua. Thirteen go through if there is an equal placing.","Te Matatini draw: all qualified kapa haka are drawn into the pools. The previous finalists are seeded (placings 1 to 12 seeded, 13 to 20 spread across the four pools, 21 and beyond drawn at random). The order of performance in Te Matangirua is drawn at random just after the Top 12 announcement.","If a kapa haka withdraws, no other kapa haka can replace it. If it withdraws after the draw, the other kapa haka move up in the order of performance.","Equal placings in a pool: the kapa haka with the highest marks across the total of all aggregate disciplines goes to Te Matangirua. If still equal, the higher mark in these disciplines decides, in this order: Te Kairangi o te Reo, Waiata ā-ringa, Whakaeke, Whakawātea, Mōteatea, Poi, Haka.","Equal placings in Te Matangirua: the highest marks across all aggregate disciplines decide Te Toa Whakaihuwaka. If still equal, the kapa haka stay equal winners.","The Te Matangirua placings decide the seeded placings of the Top 12 for the next national festival."]},{"Key":"points","Title":"Scoring and Penalties","Items":["Each aggregate discipline is marked out of a maximum of 100 points.","Each aggregate discipline has five judges. The highest and the lowest marks are removed, and the three middle marks are added and divided by three (the Olympic system).","Each non-aggregate discipline has three judges. Their three marks are added to give the final mark.","Judges can give half marks or full marks.","Equal placing in a non-aggregate discipline is decided by where the kapa haka placed overall in the pools. Scrutineers return to confirm the top 3 placings.","Equal placing in a discipline for a taonga: the kapa haka with a perfect score (5 x 100) wins, before the Olympic system is applied. If still equal, they are equal.","Time: all seven compulsory disciplines must be completed within 25 minutes. A kapa haka that goes over loses 5 points from the aggregate total in the pool competition only.","Start time is the first utterance, sound or movement. Finish time is the last utterance, sound or movement.","Waiata Tira must be completed within 4 minutes. A kapa haka that goes over loses 2 points from Waiata Tira. No minutes carry over from Waiata Tira to the compulsory disciplines.","Reporting: a kapa haka must be ready at the performers' holding area 10 minutes before its scheduled or revised time. Late reporting loses 5 points from the aggregate total in the pool competition only.","A kapa haka that is not on stage 10 minutes after its scheduled time is disqualified.","The late reporting penalty applies only if the kapa haka does not tell the Back Stage Manager about its late arrival in a satisfactory way.","A late script loses 2 points from the aggregate total in the pool competition. Late supporting material also loses 2 points.","An unauthorised musical instrument loses 2 points from each discipline where it is used.","A judge who gives a kapa haka help that is not available to all kapa haka (such as information on criteria, advice, or help in composing items) is relieved of judging duties, and that kapa haka loses ten competition points in each item the judge was involved in."]},{"Key":"judging","Title":"Judging","Items":["A judge (kaiwhiriwhiri) cannot judge if they did not attend the planned wānanga (training) for judges before the festival.","The full National Board confirms the Chief Judge. The Chief Judge takes part in confirming the 35 judges plus up to 15 back-up judges.","Judges are not automatically given an aggregate item for two rohe selections. Criteria decide who judges the aggregate and non-aggregate items. A judge can judge one aggregate item and possibly one non-aggregate item.","No judge can help any kapa haka that takes part in the festival.","Composer names are removed from the scripts that go to the judges.","Te Matatini's decision on the overall placings of kapa haka is final."]},{"Key":"conduct","Title":"Conduct, Instruments and Compliance","Items":["A kapa haka can start from the left, the centre or the right of the performance area.","No more than two acoustic stringed instruments can be used at one time during a performance.","Traditional Māori wind instruments (taonga pūoro) and traditional Māori percussion instruments can be used.","Any musical instrument not listed needs the approval of the Te Matatini National Office.","No kapa haka can use any sound amplification system other than the one provided for the festival. A kapa haka that does so is not eligible or is disqualified.","Unless agreed otherwise, every kapa haka must get all approvals and clearances for any material it performs that is not its own or is under copyright. All copyright liability is on the kapa haka and not on Te Matatini.","All kapa haka in the pool competition and the finals must follow all broadcasting agreements. A kapa haka that does not is disqualified from the festival.","Except for the conductor or choir master, no performer can enter the stage during a kapa haka performance.","If there is a serious health and safety incident or a major incident on stage, the kapa haka can replace the affected performer once that person is safely removed. The leader, with the Head Judge and the Back Stage Manager, can choose to restart the programme from the beginning or from the item that was being performed."]},{"Key":"complaints","Title":"Protests and Disputes","Items":["The Te Matatini National Board appoints a Disputes Resolution Committee for the competition. Its decisions are final.","The committee only accepts disputes from kapa haka leaders, National Board delegates and Te Matatini management.","Other disputes about the competition rules must be lodged within 30 minutes of the incident.","A dispute that could change the results for the Top 12 finalists must be lodged within 30 minutes of the time the kapa signed and received its results, and before any public announcement.","The committee can look at disputes raised after the results are announced in public, but this does not change the results.","Each kapa haka receives its own results at one place within 30 minutes of the final performance. Results are strictly confidential. A team leader or verified representative signs and records the time of receipt. The master sheet of marks is given after the announcement of the finalists.","A kapa haka has 30 minutes from its signed receipt of results to lodge a formal written dispute about a judge's comment or mark, or a collation error. It goes to the Chairperson or Competition Manager before any public result.","If the committee decides, from the master score sheet, that a mark change would not change the final standings, the matter goes to the Judges' Debrief and the results stay as they are.","A kapa haka can dispute judges' marks or comments within the competition timeline.","The dispute form asks for: name of the kapa, name and phone number of the leader, delegate or representative, the nature of the dispute (judges' marks, judges' comments, rules, other) and the dispute details."]},{"Key":"prizes","Title":"Awards","Items":["All awards (taonga) are announced and presented after the finals at the prize-giving. All taonga winners are decided from the pool competition.","Te Toa Whakaihuwaka, the overall aggregate winner, is announced at the end of the prize-giving. It carries a trophy.","Every kapa haka gets a participation certificate at the prize-giving.","Each compulsory discipline has its own award: Whakaeke, Mōteatea, Waiata ā-ringa, Poi, Haka, Whakawātea and Te Kairangi o Te Mita o Te Reo.","Non-aggregate awards: Waiata Tira (optional), Manukura Tāne (male leader), Manukura Wahine (female leader), Kākahu (costume), Titonga Hou for Mōteatea, Waiata ā-ringa, Poi and Haka (original composition), Titonga Waiata Hou, and Te Kairangi o Te Reo ā-tuhi (written te reo).","The Manukura Tāne and Manukura Wahine are the leaders who are introduced before the performance starts."]},{"Key":"other","Title":"Other Information (Te Matatini senior festival rules)","Items":["The Te Matatini National Festival rules also set these entry rules for senior kapa haka. They are not stated for the secondary schools event; ask your regional organiser.","Te Matatini senior: all regional competitions must be finished by 30 June of the year before the national festival.","Te Matatini senior: each rohe selects kapa haka by a ratio: 2 to 4 kapa haka = 1, 5 to 8 = 2, 9 to 12 = 3, 13 to 16 = 4, 17 to 20 = 5, 21 or more = 6.","Te Matatini senior: a secondary school, kura kaupapa or kura ā-iwi kapa haka that performs at a regional competition is not counted in the rohe ratio.","Te Matatini senior: a kapa haka that has competed in a regional competition in another rohe is not counted in the ratio.","Te Matatini senior: each kapa haka has at least 24 and at most 40 performers in the compulsory and optional disciplines.","Te Matatini senior: performers must be 14 years or older on the date of the regional competition and of the national performance. Kapa haka leaders check ages if there is a protest.","Te Matatini senior: at most 30 percent of the performers on stage can be enrolled at a secondary school, kura kaupapa or kura ā-iwi.","Te Matatini senior: each kapa haka must have male and female performers.","Te Matatini senior: a performer can perform for only one kapa haka in one qualifying regional competition in a competition cycle. Composite kapa haka can use two performers who perform more than once, if registered with the National Office before the competition starts.","Te Matatini senior: performers who were in a non-qualifying kapa haka at a regional competition can perform with any qualifying kapa haka. People who did not perform at a regional competition can perform at the national festival.","Te Matatini senior: performers can change between the pool competition and the finals within the same kapa haka.","Te Matatini senior: each of the awards in the competition has a named taonga (trophy) given by a sponsor or in memory of a person. These names are not listed here.","Te Matatini senior: rohe delegates must follow the competition rules. Where a rohe rule conflicts with them, the competition rules win."]}],"Documents":[{"Title":"Ngā Ture o te Whakataetae ā-Motu (Te Matatini competition rules)","Url":"https://www.tematatini.co.nz/assets/Uploads/NGA-TURE-O-TE-WHAKATAETAE-A-MOTU.pdf"},{"Title":"Ngā Kapa Haka Kura Tuarua 2026 performance schedule","Url":"https://wananga.ac.nz/media/ptwpzcbi/performance-schedule-2026.jpg"}]}`),i=[],o=[],n=[{Title:"Whakaeke",Source:"Te Matatini, Nga Ture o te Whakataetae a-Motu (Competition Rules for the Te Matatini National Festival), rule 4.1, 4.1.1, 4.1.2",Sections:{About:["Compulsory aggregate discipline: the entrance item."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["The kapa haka can start from the left, the centre or the right of the performance area.","Start time is the first utterance, sound or movement."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:["If Waiata Tira is performed, it comes before the Whakaeke."]},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Compulsory aggregate discipline: the entrance item.

## Rules
- The kapa haka can start from the left, the centre or the right of the performance area.
- Start time is the first utterance, sound or movement.

## Notes for participants
- If Waiata Tira is performed, it comes before the Whakaeke.`},{Title:"Moteatea",Source:"Te Matatini, Nga Ture o te Whakataetae a-Motu (Competition Rules for the Te Matatini National Festival), rules 4.1, 4.3, 4.4, 4.4.1",Sections:{About:["Compulsory aggregate discipline: Mōteatea (traditional chant)."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Titonga Hou (original composition) for Mōteatea can be awarded for both lyrics and music.","An original composition must be authenticated in writing, signed by the composer or composers (lyrics or music) and countersigned by the kapa haka leaders, as the original composition that will be performed. Composer names are removed from the scripts for the judges.","A kapa haka that does not meet this is not eligible for the Titonga Hou category."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Compulsory aggregate discipline: Mōteatea (traditional chant).

## Rules
- Titonga Hou (original composition) for Mōteatea can be awarded for both lyrics and music.
- An original composition must be authenticated in writing, signed by the composer or composers (lyrics or music) and countersigned by the kapa haka leaders, as the original composition that will be performed. Composer names are removed from the scripts for the judges.
- A kapa haka that does not meet this is not eligible for the Titonga Hou category.`},{Title:"Waiata a-ringa",Source:"Te Matatini, Nga Ture o te Whakataetae a-Motu (Competition Rules for the Te Matatini National Festival), rules 4.1, 4.3, 4.4, 4.4.1",Sections:{About:["Compulsory aggregate discipline: Waiata ā-ringa (action song)."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Titonga Hou (original composition) for Waiata ā-ringa can be awarded for both lyrics and music.","An original composition must be authenticated in writing, signed by the composer or composers (lyrics or music) and countersigned by the kapa haka leaders. Composer names are removed from the scripts for the judges.","A kapa haka that does not meet this is not eligible for the Titonga Hou category."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Compulsory aggregate discipline: Waiata ā-ringa (action song).

## Rules
- Titonga Hou (original composition) for Waiata ā-ringa can be awarded for both lyrics and music.
- An original composition must be authenticated in writing, signed by the composer or composers (lyrics or music) and countersigned by the kapa haka leaders. Composer names are removed from the scripts for the judges.
- A kapa haka that does not meet this is not eligible for the Titonga Hou category.`},{Title:"Poi",Source:"Te Matatini, Nga Ture o te Whakataetae a-Motu (Competition Rules for the Te Matatini National Festival), rules 4.1, 4.3, 4.4, 4.4.1",Sections:{About:["Compulsory aggregate discipline: Poi."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Titonga Hou (original composition) for Poi can be awarded for both lyrics and music.","An original composition must be authenticated in writing, signed by the composer or composers (lyrics or music) and countersigned by the kapa haka leaders. Composer names are removed from the scripts for the judges.","A kapa haka that does not meet this is not eligible for the Titonga Hou category."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Compulsory aggregate discipline: Poi.

## Rules
- Titonga Hou (original composition) for Poi can be awarded for both lyrics and music.
- An original composition must be authenticated in writing, signed by the composer or composers (lyrics or music) and countersigned by the kapa haka leaders. Composer names are removed from the scripts for the judges.
- A kapa haka that does not meet this is not eligible for the Titonga Hou category.`},{Title:"Haka",Source:"Te Matatini, Nga Ture o te Whakataetae a-Motu (Competition Rules for the Te Matatini National Festival), rules 4.1, 4.3, 4.4, 4.4.1",Sections:{About:["Compulsory aggregate discipline: Haka."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Titonga Hou (original composition) for Haka can be awarded for both lyrics and music.","An original composition must be authenticated in writing, signed by the composer or composers (lyrics or music) and countersigned by the kapa haka leaders. Composer names are removed from the scripts for the judges.","A kapa haka that does not meet this is not eligible for the Titonga Hou category."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Compulsory aggregate discipline: Haka.

## Rules
- Titonga Hou (original composition) for Haka can be awarded for both lyrics and music.
- An original composition must be authenticated in writing, signed by the composer or composers (lyrics or music) and countersigned by the kapa haka leaders. Composer names are removed from the scripts for the judges.
- A kapa haka that does not meet this is not eligible for the Titonga Hou category.`},{Title:"Whakawatea",Source:"Te Matatini, Nga Ture o te Whakataetae a-Motu (Competition Rules for the Te Matatini National Festival), rules 4.1, 4.1.3, 4.1.4",Sections:{About:["Compulsory aggregate discipline: the exit item."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["At the end of the Whakawātea, the kapa haka movement must show an exit to the left, the centre or the right.","Finish time is the last utterance, sound or movement."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Compulsory aggregate discipline: the exit item.

## Rules
- At the end of the Whakawātea, the kapa haka movement must show an exit to the left, the centre or the right.
- Finish time is the last utterance, sound or movement.`},{Title:"Te Kairangi o Te Mita o Te Reo",Source:"Te Matatini, Nga Ture o te Whakataetae a-Motu (Competition Rules for the Te Matatini National Festival), rules 2a, 4.1 and Part A 1b.vi",Sections:{About:["Compulsory aggregate discipline: the quality of delivery of te reo Māori (Māori language)."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:null,Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:["This is the first discipline used to break a tie between equal placings in a pool."],Notes:["A separate non-aggregate award, Te Kairangi o Te Reo ā-tuhi, is given for written te reo."]},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Compulsory aggregate discipline: the quality of delivery of te reo Māori (Māori language).

## Points
- This is the first discipline used to break a tie between equal placings in a pool.

## Notes for participants
- A separate non-aggregate award, Te Kairangi o Te Reo ā-tuhi, is given for written te reo.`},{Title:"Waiata Tira (Optional)",Source:"Te Matatini, Nga Ture o te Whakataetae a-Motu (Competition Rules for the Te Matatini National Festival), rules 4.2, 4.2.1, 4.2.2, 4.2.3, 5.2, 5.2.1, 2.2.2",Sections:{About:["Optional discipline. It is not part of the aggregate."],TimeAndFormat:["Must be completed within a maximum of 4 minutes.","Start time is the first utterance, sound or movement. Finish time is the last utterance, sound or movement.","No minutes carry over from Waiata Tira to the compulsory disciplines."],Rules:["Must be performed before the Whakaeke.","A conductor or choir master can be extra to the performers but must not perform in the compulsory disciplines."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:["Three judges. Their marks are added to give the final mark."],Points:["A kapa haka that goes over 4 minutes loses 2 points from Waiata Tira."],Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Optional discipline. It is not part of the aggregate.

## Time and format
- Must be completed within a maximum of 4 minutes.
- Start time is the first utterance, sound or movement. Finish time is the last utterance, sound or movement.
- No minutes carry over from Waiata Tira to the compulsory disciplines.

## Rules
- Must be performed before the Whakaeke.
- A conductor or choir master can be extra to the performers but must not perform in the compulsory disciplines.

## Judging
- Three judges. Their marks are added to give the final mark.

## Points
- A kapa haka that goes over 4 minutes loses 2 points from Waiata Tira.`}],r=[],s=[{Key:"EventDates",Kind:"date",Label:"Competition dates",Value:"2026-06-29 to 2026-07-03",Source:"Te Whare Wananga o Awanuiarangi event page, Nga Kapa Haka Kura Tuarua 2026"},{Key:"Venue",Kind:"address",Label:"Competition venue",Value:{Name:"Mercury Baypark",Address:"",City:"Tauranga",MapUrl:"",Directions:""},Source:"Te Whare Wananga o Awanuiarangi event page, Nga Kapa Haka Kura Tuarua 2026"},{Key:"Host",Kind:"text",Label:"Host of this edition",Value:"Mataatua Waka, in Tauranga Moana",Source:"Te Whare Wananga o Awanuiarangi event page, Nga Kapa Haka Kura Tuarua 2026"},{Key:"EditionFormat",Kind:"text",Label:"Format of this edition",Value:"Monday 29 June, event pohiri; Tuesday 30 June to Thursday 2 July, preliminary performances; Friday 3 July, finals day",Source:"Te Whare Wananga o Awanuiarangi event page, Nga Kapa Haka Kura Tuarua 2026"},{Key:"Livestream",Kind:"link",Label:"Livestream of the performances",Value:"https://www.maoriplus.co.nz/show/nga-kapa-haka-kura-tuarua-o-aotearoa-2026",Source:"Te Whare Wananga o Awanuiarangi event page, Nga Kapa Haka Kura Tuarua 2026"},{Key:"PerformanceSchedule",Kind:"link",Label:"Performance schedule (image)",Value:"https://wananga.ac.nz/media/ptwpzcbi/performance-schedule-2026.jpg",Source:"Te Whare Wananga o Awanuiarangi event page, Nga Kapa Haka Kura Tuarua 2026"},{Key:"Announcements",Kind:"news",Label:"News printed on the event pages",Value:[{Date:"2026-06-29",Title:"Nationals 2026",Text:"Te Tawharau o Nga Waka, the National Secondary Schools Kapa Haka Competition, is held at Mercury Baypark, Tauranga, from 29 June to 3 July 2026, hosted by Mataatua Waka. It brings together the top qualifying kura from every rohe.",Url:"https://wananga.ac.nz/news-and-events/events/nga-kapa-haka-kura-tuarua-2026/"},{Date:"2025-05-23",Title:"Past regional event: Ngati Kahungunu",Text:"Kura Tuarua Regionals on 23 May 2025, 8:00am to 5:00pm, Napier Municipal Theatre (2025 edition, shown as a past example).",Url:"https://www.tematatini.co.nz/events/te-matau-a-maui-kapa-haka-kura-tuarua-regionals/"},{Date:"2025-06-19",Title:"Past regional event: Te Arawa",Text:"Kura Tuarua Regionals on 19 June 2025, 8:00am to 5:00pm, Rotorua Energy Events Centre (2025 edition, shown as a past example).",Url:"https://www.tematatini.co.nz/events/te-arawa-kapa-haka-kura-tuarua-regionals"}],Source:"Te Whare Wananga o Awanuiarangi event page, Nga Kapa Haka Kura Tuarua 2026; Te Matatini regional event pages for Kura Tuarua Regionals"}],l=[{Claim:"Disciplines, scoring, time and penalty, scripts, instruments, copyright, results and dispute rules are from Te Matatini's Ngā Ture o te Whakataetae ā-Motu (22 pages), read in full. They are the Te Matatini National Festival rules, which the template follows because the secondary schools event publishes none.",SourceType:"Primary - Te Matatini competition rules (senior festival)",Source:"Te Matatini, Nga Ture o te Whakataetae a-Motu (Competition Rules for the Te Matatini National Festival)",Url:"https://www.tematatini.co.nz/assets/Uploads/NGA-TURE-O-TE-WHAKATAETAE-A-MOTU.pdf"},{Claim:"The 2026 edition dates, venue, host, format and livestream are from the Te Whare Wānanga o Awanuiārangi (sponsor) event page. Two 2025 regional dates are from Te Matatini's regional event pages. Neither page prints rules, age limits, group size, fees or an entry deadline.",SourceType:"Secondary - sponsor and regional event pages",Source:"Te Whare Wananga o Awanuiarangi event page, Nga Kapa Haka Kura Tuarua 2026; Te Matatini regional event pages for Kura Tuarua Regionals",Url:"https://wananga.ac.nz/news-and-events/events/nga-kapa-haka-kura-tuarua-2026/"}],u={TemplateKey:e,Year:2026,Note:a,Page:t,ParticipantTypes:i,AgeGroups:o,Competitions:n,CompetitionAgeGroups:r,Variables:s,Evidence:l};export{o as AgeGroups,r as CompetitionAgeGroups,n as Competitions,l as Evidence,a as Note,t as Page,i as ParticipantTypes,e as TemplateKey,s as Variables,h as Year,u as default};
